---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PERSON_ID } from '../../lib/schema';

const service = {
	'@type': 'Service',
	'@id': 'https://okarthur.com/review/#service',
	name: 'Independent review',
	serviceType:
		'Independent review of an infrastructure decision before it is confirmed',
	description:
		'A second opinion on a digital, data or AI investment, or on a decision that commits more than one development. Fixed scope, a fixed end date, and one written opinion from someone with no stake in the outcome.',
	url: 'https://okarthur.com/review/',
	provider: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
	areaServed: [
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Arab Emirates' },
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Kingdom' },
		{ '@type': 'Place', name: 'Gulf Cooperation Council' },
	],
	audience: {
		'@type': 'Audience',
		audienceType:
			'Infrastructure owners, developers and major capital programmes',
	},
};

/* The section and the FAQPage node are both built from this, so the answers
   Google reads and the answers on the page cannot drift apart. */
const questions = [
	{
		q: 'Is this an audit?',
		a: "An audit tests compliance against a standard. A review gives an opinion on whether a decision is supported by its evidence. That evidence is usually a familiar set of documents, such as a business case, a supplier's proposal, a pilot's results and a set of benefits claims. The review tests whether those documents answer the questions the decision turns on. It carries no certification and can run alongside formal assurance.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Can a decision that has already been made be reviewed?',
		a: 'A decision already made can still be reviewed, such as a completed supplier selection or a platform that has been funded. The question changes from whether to commit to what must now be true for the investment to pay back. The method is the same.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can you review a procurement before it goes to market?',
		a: 'This is one of the most useful moments to look. By the time bids arrive, most of the outcome has been set by what the tender asked for and how it will be scored. A review at this point examines the technical scope and the evaluation criteria. It asks whether the contract should be bundled or decoupled. It also asks whether what is being bought fits the architecture and data it will depend on. Fixing a tender costs far less than managing a wrong award.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does this need new reporting from my teams?',
		a: 'No team is asked for new reporting. The review works from the material each development already holds. Nothing has to leave your organisation. The review can run on your own systems under your own confidentiality terms if that is simpler.',
	},
	{
		q: 'You spent years on the supplier side. How is the review independent?',
		a: 'For 17 years I helped build the evidence that sellers put in front of infrastructure owners. I know what that material is written to show and which questions it is not built to answer. Independence is written into each engagement. There is no delivery work behind the opinion. Any interest is declared before work starts.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do you take supplier-side work?',
		a: 'I take no supplier-side work on anything under review. If a conflict cannot be cleanly separated, I decline the engagement.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does it cost?',
		a: 'The fee is fixed and proposed after a first call. There is no published rate because scope is agreed for each decision. Once the fee is agreed it does not change. There is no time-and-materials element.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Why one person and not a firm?',
		a: 'A firm that reviews a proposal is often in a position to sell what follows, such as the implementation or the programme office. I have seen that from the inside. A review here has no delivery team behind it and no follow-on work it is priced to win. The opinion is the whole of what you buy.',
	},
];

const faq = {
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	'@id': 'https://okarthur.com/review/#faq',
	mainEntity: questions.map(({ q, a }) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name: q,
		acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
	})),
};
---

<Base
	title="Independent review"
	description="A second opinion on a decision in infrastructure, before you commit. Fixed scope, fixed end date, one written opinion."
	schema={[service, faq]}
>
	<div class="page-head">
		<h1 class="page-title">Independent review</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			A second opinion on a decision, before you commit.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="prose numbered">
		<p>
			Digital and AI investments are usually approved on evidence prepared by
			people who gain from the approval. The supplier wants the contract and
			the delivery team wants the programme. Their evidence tends to show what
			each of them needs it to show, without anyone being dishonest. A review
			tests that evidence on the owner's behalf.
		</p>
		<p>
			A decision that affects several developments has a different problem.
			Each development reports against its own budget and programme. What the
			decision means for the others appears in nobody's report. It usually
			comes to light when it is too late to change. The review asks whether
			the portfolio still lands within its range after the decision. If it
			does not, the review sets out what can still be moved.
		</p>

		<h2 id="when-it-applies">When it applies</h2>
		<p>A review fits a decision point of one of two kinds.</p>
		<h3>A digital or AI commitment</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				A supplier's proposal for a digital or AI system, before you accept
				it.
			</li>
			<li>
				A digital twin or data platform, before you fund it or fund it again.
			</li>
			<li>A digital asset, before you accept handover into operation.</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>A decision that affects the portfolio</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				A change of plan at group level, such as a pause or a re-phasing,
				before anyone has worked out what it releases or delays elsewhere.
			</li>
			<li>
				A portfolio forecast to miss its range, where the owner needs to know
				which developments can still make up the difference.
			</li>
			<li>
				A business case approved some time ago whose assumptions have since
				moved, such as a utility connection or a handover date next door.
			</li>
			<li>
				A committee choosing which phases to fund within a limit, where each
				case was made on its own and none has been compared with the others.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="what-is-reviewed">What is reviewed</h2>
		<p>
			The review works from material that already exists. That is usually the
			business case, the supplier's proposal, the information requirements
			and any assurance evidence. A small number of interviews are added,
			agreed and named before work starts.
		</p>
		<p>
			For a decision that affects several developments, the material is the
			programmes and contracts each development already holds. Nobody is
			asked for new reporting. Much of the value comes from setting those
			documents side by side. A business case that assumes a March handover,
			next to a programme that now shows September, is the kind of gap a
			review is there to find.
		</p>
		<p>The decision is then tested against five questions.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>What decision does this evidence actually support.</li>
			<li>What would have to be true for the claim to hold.</li>
			<li>What breaks it.</li>
			<li>What does it cost to keep true after year one.</li>
			<li>
				What else it commits, and the date after which that cannot be changed.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="what-you-receive">What you receive</h2>
		<p>
			You receive one written opinion, short enough to put in front of a board
			and specific enough to act on. It sets out what the evidence supports
			and what it does not. It states the conditions under which the
			investment can be defended. It lists the risks that matter, ranked by
			severity. A one-page summary comes with it for your own paper. I present
			the opinion once to the sponsor or the board.
		</p>
		<p>
			Where the decision affects several developments, the opinion also sets
			out what it commits elsewhere and by when. It shows which options are
			still open. It is about the decision and does not apportion blame
			between teams.
		</p>
		<p>
			Every opinion carries a reliance statement on its front page. It limits
			the opinion to the decision reviewed, the material examined and the date
			of issue. An opinion, like a digital twin, is <a
				href="/notes/what-makes-a-digital-twin-worth-relying-on/"
				>worth relying on only within stated limits</a
			>.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<svg
				viewBox="23 0 435 360"
				role="img"
				aria-labelledby="fig-reliance-title fig-reliance-desc"
			>
				<title id="fig-reliance-title">The front page of an opinion</title>
				<desc id="fig-reliance-desc">
					A page headed Independent review, Opinion. Near the top is a boxed
					reliance statement with three entries. They are the decision
					reviewed, the material examined and the date issued. Each is circled
					in red, and a red note says the opinion is valid within these limits
					only.
				</desc>
				<path class="fig-sheet" d="M24 352 V8 H456 V352" />
				<text class="fig-label" x="48" y="40">INDEPENDENT REVIEW</text>
				<text class="fig-label" x="432" y="40" text-anchor="end">PAGE 1</text>
				<text class="fig-title" x="48" y="74">Opinion</text>
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="48" y="88" width="230" height="6" />
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="48" y="100" width="170" height="6" />

				<rect class="fig-box" x="48" y="122" width="384" height="148" />
				<text class="fig-label" x="64" y="146">RELIANCE STATEMENT</text>

				<text class="fig-text" x="64" y="178">Decision reviewed</text>
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="214" y="170" width="190" height="6" />
				<text class="fig-text" x="64" y="210">Material examined</text>
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="214" y="202" width="160" height="6" />
				<text class="fig-text" x="64" y="242">Date issued</text>
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="214" y="234" width="90" height="6" />

				<path
					class="fig-pen"
					d="M58 172 C58 160 120 158 186 160 C204 161 206 179 190 183 C150 189 76 188 60 181 C55 179 56 175 60 172"
				/>
				<path
					class="fig-pen"
					d="M57 204 C58 192 124 190 188 192 C205 193 206 211 190 215 C148 221 74 220 59 213 C54 211 55 207 60 204"
				/>
				<path
					class="fig-pen"
					d="M57 236 C58 225 110 223 150 224 C166 225 167 243 151 247 C120 252 72 251 59 245 C54 243 55 239 60 236"
				/>
				<path class="fig-pen" d="M418 168 C428 190 428 222 418 246" />
				<path class="fig-pen" d="M412 240 L418 248 L426 241" />

				<text class="fig-pen-text" x="48" y="298">
					Valid within these limits only.
				</text>

				<rect class="fig-bar" x="48" y="318" width="384" height="6" />
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="48" y="332" width="350" height="6" />
				<rect class="fig-bar" x="48" y="346" width="370" height="6" />
			</svg>
			<figcaption>
				Figure 1. The front page of every opinion. The reliance statement limits
				it to the decision reviewed, the material examined and the date of
				issue.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<h2 id="how-it-runs">How it runs</h2>
		<p>
			Scope and end date are agreed before work starts. A review typically
			takes 3 to 4 weeks from kickoff. The fee is fixed after a first call and
			does not change. There is no time-and-materials billing.
		</p>

		<h2 id="independence">The independence rule</h2>
		<p>
			On any review I take no delivery work and no supplier-side work. I have
			no stake in the outcome. Any interest is declared before the engagement
			starts. If a conflict cannot be cleanly separated, I decline the
			engagement.
		</p>

		<h2 id="out-of-scope">Out of scope</h2>
		<p>
			A review does not include implementation or delivery resource. It does
			not run a supplier selection, though a completed selection can be
			reviewed. It carries no certification or regulatory sign-off. The
			supplier's technical work is examined and is not redone.
		</p>

		<h2 id="questions">Common questions</h2>
		{
			questions.map(({ q, a }) => (
				<Fragment>
					<h3>{q}</h3>
					<p>{a}</p>
				</Fragment>
			))
		}

		<h2 id="beyond-the-review">Beyond the review</h2>
		<p>
			Continuing advice can follow a review where an owner wants it. The other
			ways to engage are described under <a href="/work/">Work</a>. If a review is not
			the right shape for you, say so in your first message.
		</p>

		<h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="/contact/">Tell me the decision you are facing</a> and when it
			has to be made.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>
